<h2 class="font-headline-md text-headline-md text-primary mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
</div>
<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the difference between gingivitis and periodontitis?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Gingivitis is reversible gum inflammation caused by bacterial plaque — gums are red, swollen, and bleed easily but the underlying bone is intact. Periodontitis is irreversible — the infection has destroyed the bone and ligament attaching the tooth to the jaw, causing gum recession, pocket formation, and eventual tooth mobility and loss. Gingivitis progresses to periodontitis when left untreated.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can gum infection spread to other parts of the body?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — chronic gum (periodontal) disease is associated with systemic health effects. Oral bacteria from infected gums can enter the bloodstream and have been linked to cardiovascular disease, diabetes complications, respiratory infections, and adverse pregnancy outcomes. Treating gum infection is not just about saving teeth — it has broader health implications.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How do I prevent gum infection from recurring?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Prevention combines professional care with daily oral hygiene. Professional care: regular dental cleanings every 6 months (more frequently for periodontal patients). Daily hygiene: brushing twice daily with fluoride toothpaste, flossing once daily, and using an antiseptic mouthwash if recommended. Quitting smoking is the single most impactful lifestyle change — smoking is a major risk factor for gum disease.</div>
</details>
</div>
</div>
